{Source: Hastings and St Leonards Observer - Saturday 03 October 1953. "Here
is another interesting position from the Hastings 1929/ 1930 Christmas
Congress. Mr. S. F. Dalladay, still an active [Hastings] club member, had just
agreed a draw with his opponent. At this moment Capablanca, who was taking a
walk between moves of his own game, stopped to look. At rook and pawn endings
Capablanca has never been excelled, and, after a single glance, he pointed out
that White had a win. It is a technique which crops up quite frequently. What
is it?   Hastings and St Leonards Observer - Saturday 17 October 1953: As for
Mr. Dalladay’s same. Capablanca said that the line was to play P—B5,
sacrificing a pawn; the white king would be able, then, to reach the
im­portant square B4. It is curious how powerless black is to prevent the
white king thrusting through to K5. from which square it dominates the ending.
"  However, Stockfish is unimpressed:} 1. f5 exf5 ({After} 1... gxf5 $2 2. Kf4
{and Capa's idea works}) 2. Rd7 Ra1 (2... Re6 3. Rxd5 Kf7 {looks hard to beat
too.}) 3. Rxd5 Rg1 4. Kf4 Kf7 $1 {and there's nothing very convincing for
White.} 1/2-1/2

